## Setup and Installation

### System Requirements

**1. Python 3.10 or later**

To install Python, search "Python install" and follow the official steps at [python.org](https://www.python.org/downloads/).

**2. Enabling Script Execution (Windows only)**

Search for "PowerShell" in the Start menu, open it, and run:
```
Set-ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned -Scope CurrentUser
```

---

### Installation

Go to the following link:
```
https://github.com/really-lazy-cat/chat-overseer
```
Click the button labelled **"<> Code"**, then click **"Download ZIP"**.

Extract the ZIP file into your desired folder.

---

### Setup

#### 1. Create the Virtual Environment

Open a terminal in the project folder (right-click -> "Open in Terminal") and run:
```
python -m venv .overseer_venv
```
If this fails with "Python is not installed", revisit the System Requirements above.

#### 2. Activate the Virtual Environment

Run this command. Note that you must do this **every time before running the script**:
```
.overseer_venv/Scripts/activate.ps1
```

#### 3. Install Necessary Libraries

With the virtual environment active, run:
```
pip install -r requirements.txt
```
If this fails with "pip is not installed", reinstall Python.

#### 4. Fill In Required Constants

Before running the script, open `chat_overseer_v4.py` and fill in the following values near the top of the file:

| Constant | What to put here |
|---|---|
| `BITRIX_WEBHOOK` | Your Bitrix24 webhook URL |
| `NOTIFICATION_FROM` | The Gmail address notifications are sent **from** |
| `NOTIFICATION_TO` | The email address notifications are sent **to** |

#### 5. Get the Gmail API Credentials

Go to [https://console.cloud.google.com](https://console.cloud.google.com) and log in with the account you want to send notifications from.

Search for **"Gmail API"** and enable it. Then go to the **Credentials** tab and create credentials:
- Choose **OAuth Client ID**
- Application Type: **Desktop App**
- Name: anything you like

Download the credentials as a JSON file, rename it to `email.json`, and place it in:
```
sensitive_info/credentials/email.json
```

#### 6. Set Up the Employee Links File

Create a CSV file at `sensitive_info/employee_links.csv`. It should have two columns:
- Column 1: Employee full name (exactly as it appears in Bitrix)
- Column 2: A link for that employee (e.g. a Teams or email link)

---

### First Run: Credentials Prompts

When you run the script for the first time, you will be prompted **twice**:

**First prompt: ChatApp credentials:**

You will be asked for:
- Email/username of the ChatApp admin account
- Password
- App ID (found on the ChatApp dashboard)

These are saved to `sensitive_info/credentials/chatapp_request.json` and will not be asked again.

**Second prompt: Gmail access:**

A browser window will open asking you to log in and grant the script access to send emails. Log in with the same Gmail account whose credentials file you placed in step 5. The access token will be saved to `sensitive_info/credentials/email_token.json`.

---

### Running the Script

With the virtual environment active, run:
```
python chat_overseer_v4.py
```

The script will then run continuously, monitoring all messages. To stop it, press `Ctrl + C`.

---

## Making Changes

### 1. Adding or Removing Keywords

Find the `KEYWORDS` list near the top of the script (around line 60). Add or remove items from the list, keeping each keyword in quotes and separated by commas.

### 2. Changing the Fuzzy Match Sensitivity

Find the following line near the top of the script:
```python
FUZZY_THRESHOLD = 85
```
This is a percentage. 85 means a word must be at least 85% similar to a keyword to be flagged. Lower this number to catch more near-matches; raise it to reduce false positives.

### 3. Adding or Removing Regex Patterns

Find the `REGEX_PATTERNS` dictionary near the top of the script. Each entry is a label and a regex pattern. Add new ones in the same format, or remove existing ones.

### 4. Adding or Removing Payment Domains

Find the `PAYMENT_DOMAINS` list near the top of the script and add or remove domain strings as needed.

### 5. Changing the Warning Message

Find `FileViolationStrategy` in the script and look for the `warning_text` variable inside it. Edit the text as needed.

> **Note:** Do not remove the phrase "CBUAE regulations" from the warning message. The script uses this phrase to identify and skip its own warning messages when scanning new messages. If you change it, find the line `if text and "CBUAE regulations" in text` in `MessagePipeline.process` and update it to match.

### 6. Changing the Notification Email Content

Find the `send_notification` method inside `GmailClient` and edit the `subject` or `body` variables inside it. There are two branches: one for file violations and one for text violations.

### 7. Changing the Email Address Used

Update `NOTIFICATION_FROM` and `NOTIFICATION_TO` near the top of the script. If you change the sending account (`NOTIFICATION_FROM`), you will also need to redo the Gmail API credentials setup (steps 5 and 6 above) with the new account.

